# Installation

@lede
The reference compiler is a single Rust binary. Build it from source.
@end

## Requirements

- Rust 1.85 or later (workspace uses edition 2024).
- A POSIX-y shell, or PowerShell on Windows.
- Roughly 200 MB of disk for the build cache.

That is the entire dependency footprint. There is no Node, no Python, no
Docker, no system package manager step. The compiler is one binary; the
docs tooling is one more.

## Build from source

```bash
git clone https://github.com/kirillleventcov/brief
cd brief
cargo build --release
```

The release build produces two binaries under `target/release/`:

@t
| Binary | Crate | Purpose
| `brief` | `crates/brief-cli` | The compiler. Compile, convert, explain.
| `brief-web` | `crates/brief-web` | The static-site generator that built this site.

Add `target/release/` to your `PATH` or symlink the binaries somewhere
already on it.

## Verify

```bash
brief --help
brief compile --help
brief-web --help
```

If `brief --help` prints a usage banner, you are done. If it does not, the
build did not finish or `target/release/` is not on your `PATH`. Re-run
`cargo build --release` and re-check the output path.

## What you just installed

@dl
brief
: The reference compiler. Lex, parse, validate, and emit HTML, LLM, or
  JSON-AST. Also explains error codes and converts Markdown.
brief-web
: The static-site generator. Reads a directory of `.brf` files plus a
  `book.toml` and produces a navigable HTML site, with a dev server that
  live-reloads on edit.
@end

